many cloud native development teams work  with a mix of configuration systems  apis and tools to manage their  infrastructure  this mix is often difficult to  understand leading to reduced velocity  and expensive mistakes  let's look at how alice uses config  connector  for consistent gcp resource management  alice is a new engineer preparing for  her first on-call  she is also worried about having to  learn new commands to make configuration  changes  using specialized tools to solve a  plethora of problems is one of the  benefits  of the linux and microservices  approaches  but it also comes with added complexity  of having to learn  many tools to do often similar tasks  many times it turns out that these  separate components  would have been served if they allowed  you to express what you want to do  without having to focus on the specific  api to make it happen  luckily alice has a lot of experience  with k-8 tool chains  she discovers that using config  connector cloud resources are managed  consistently with k-8s  native resources like pods and services  alice realizes that she can fully  operate the cloud using  her existing k8 expertise and get ops  and that's the benefit of config  connector config connector is a  kubernetes add-on  that allows you to manage google cloud  resources through kubernetes manifests  because you can manage your google cloud  infrastructure the same way you manage  your kubernetes application  complexity and cognitive load is reduced  for developers  this means more focus where it matters  on the infrastructure  config connectors provide a collection  of kubernetes custom resource  definitions  crds and controllers the config  connector crds allow kubernetes to  create and manage google cloud resources  like a cloud sql database the same way  kubernetes developers are used to  applying kubernetes objects to their  clusters  you configure and apply objects to your  clusters  since alice is already familiar with  kubernetes tooling  and declarative workflow she is able to  use  her existing expertise to quickly ramp  up and manage the customer's workload  let's see a quick example of this in  action  alice wants to create a storage bucket  to store images  the first step is to create a resource  definition  which uses yaml to specify the storage  bucket's information  like its name and what project it's  associated with  this resource can be updated through the  kubernetes api  by updating the storage buckets yaml  file  in this case alice added a label to the  bucket and annotation to specify  other behaviors on your storage bucket  running the familiar  coop ctl commands we already know and  love like  coupe ctl apply in this case we can  create the storage bucket  now instead of having to learn another  tool like the gsutil tool  alice was able to use the knowledge she  already had to create her storage bucket  all these changes were done through the  kubernetes epi which means alice could  apply the expertise she's already built  up over time  without having to learn new tools to  manage infrastructure  that's one of the benefits of managing  gcp resources  with config connector in combination  with config sync  you also ensure the change process is  consistent through git ops  removing the risk of config drift  so that's it for config connector make  sure to check out the description for  links to more resources on it  and like and subscribe to the channel  for more videos about google cloud  thanks for watching  you
 

